Key Changes Explained
WAV Format Streaming:
Send a proper WAV header at the beginning before sending any audio data
Stream continuous PCM data chunks directly without additional headers
Set a placeholder size in the header large enough to accommodate all the audio
Opus Format Streaming:
Use OGG container format for better streaming with Opus codec
Set up a dedicated FFmpeg process that runs throughout the streaming session
Feed PCM data to FFmpeg and stream the OGG/Opus output to the client
Use -flush_packets 1 to ensure data is sent immediately for better streaming
Smooth Playback Experience:
For WAV: Mobile clients can parse the initial header and then append all subsequent raw PCM data
For Opus: The OGG container format is designed for streaming and can be played as a continuous stream in iOS/Android
These changes ensure that:
Both formats are supported as requested
Mobile clients receive properly formatted streaming audio they can play without interruption
The audio starts playing as soon as the first chunk arrives, while subsequent chunks are added seamlessly
This implementation should provide a smooth playback experience for iOS and Android clients, allowing them to play the audio as if it were a single continuous file.